<Layout
  ogType="article"
  title="How to Bid a Construction Job: 7 Steps for Subs | BuildWorkPro"
  description="How subcontractors bid a construction job: plans and specs, takeoff, burdened labor, materials, overhead and profit, a worked bid example, and the proposal that wins the work."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="mb-12">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Blog</p>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">
          How to Bid a Construction Job: A 7-Step Guide for Subcontractors
        </h1>
        <AuthorByline />
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-400">
          Published March 15, 2026 · Updated October 7, 2026 · 10 min read
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-brand-600"
      >
        <p>
          A well-built bid is the difference between winning profitable work and leaving money on
          the table. For subcontractors, the bidding process is where your entire project starts —
          get it right and you set yourself up for healthy margins. Get it wrong and you're either
          working for free or losing the job to someone who priced it tighter.
        </p>
        <p>
          This guide explains how to bid a job, step by step, from reviewing plans to submitting
          your proposal — with a worked example of the math in the middle. The documents themselves
          are separate free downloads: a
          <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">construction estimate template</a> for the cost
          side, a <a href="/templates/construction-bid-proposal/">bid proposal template</a> for what the
          GC receives, and a one-page
          <a href="/templates/construction-quote/">construction quote template</a> for small jobs. Before
          you price anything, the invitation should already be a lead on your
          <a href="/features/construction-crm/">construction sales CRM</a>
          — so you know what else is in the pipeline and whether this invite is worth estimating this
          week.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-100 p-6">
          <h2 class="mt-0">Key takeaways</h2>
          <ul class="mb-0">
            <li>
              Read the full spec book and every addendum before pricing — missed addenda are one of
              the most common ways subs leave money out of a bid.
            </li>
            <li>
              Price labor at fully burdened rates and your crews' real production rates, not
              industry averages.
            </li>
            <li>
              Get current supplier quotes with a price hold through bid day, and flag long-lead
              items in your proposal.
            </li>
            <li>
              Apply overhead (typically 8-15%) and profit (5-10%) deliberately, in the format the
              bid documents require.
            </li>
            <li>
              Spell out inclusions, exclusions, and assumptions in a clean proposal — and submit it
              well before the deadline.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="not-prose rounded-2xl border border-brand-100 bg-brand-50 p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Skip the blank page</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
            Our free <a
              href="/templates/construction-bid-proposal/"
              class="text-brand-600 underline underline-offset-2 font-medium"
              >bid proposal template</a
            >
            has the scope, price, inclusions/exclusions, and acceptance sections ready to fill in — and
            the
            <a
              href="/templates/construction-estimate/"
              class="text-brand-600 underline underline-offset-2 font-medium">estimate template</a
            > does the math behind the number. No email required.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>Estimate, bid, proposal or quote?</h2>
        <p>
          The four words get used interchangeably, but they are four different things, and keeping
          them apart makes the steps below easier to follow:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Estimate</strong> — your internal number: what the work will cost you in labor, material,
            equipment and subs. The customer never sees it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Bid</strong> — the price you offer for a defined scope, usually in competition with
            other subs: the estimate plus overhead and profit.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Proposal</strong> — the document that carries the bid: scope, inclusions, exclusions,
            assumptions, terms and an acceptance block.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Quote</strong> — a short fixed price for a small, well-defined job, often direct to
            an owner, with a validity date.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>Steps 1 through 6 build the estimate and the bid. Step 7 is the proposal.</p>

        <h2>Step 1: Review the Plans and Specifications</h2>
        <p>
          Before you touch a calculator, <a href="/blog/how-to-read-construction-plans-takeoff/"
            >read the plans</a
          > thoroughly. Go through the spec book section by section — not just your division, but adjacent
          trades too. You need to understand the full scope so you know where your work starts and stops.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pay close attention to the general conditions, special conditions, and any addenda. Missed
          addenda are one of the most common reasons subs leave money out of a bid. Check for
          phasing requirements, schedule constraints, and liquidated damages clauses that could
          affect your labor plan.
        </p>

        <h2>Step 2: Perform Your Takeoff</h2>
        <p>
          The takeoff is the foundation of your bid. Whether you're counting fixtures, measuring
          linear feet of pipe, or calculating square footage of drywall, accuracy here determines
          everything downstream.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Use consistent units.</strong> If you're measuring conduit in linear feet, keep everything
            in linear feet. Mixing units is a fast track to pricing errors.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Account for waste.</strong> Material waste factors vary by trade — 5% for copper pipe,
            10-15% for drywall, up to 20% for tile. Use the factor your crews actually experience, not
            the textbook number.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Use a count sheet built for your trade.</strong> The free trade estimate templates
            each include a takeoff laid out the way that trade measures: squares and bundles in the
            <a href="/templates/roofing-estimate/">roofing estimate template</a>, board counts in
            the
            <a href="/templates/drywall-estimate/">drywall estimate template</a>, device counts in
            the
            <a href="/templates/electrical-estimate/">electrical estimate template</a>, and fixture
            counts in the <a href="/templates/plumbing-estimate/">plumbing estimate template</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Separate by area or phase.</strong> Breaking your takeoff into zones or phases makes
            it easier to verify quantities and adjust if the <a
              href="/blog/construction-change-order-management/">scope changes</a
            > later.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Step 3: Price Your Labor</h2>
        <p>
          Labor is typically the largest cost in a subcontractor's bid, and it's where most
          estimating errors happen. Start with your crew composition — how many journeymen,
          apprentices, and helpers you'll need for each task.
        </p>
        <p>
          Calculate your fully burdened labor rate, not just the hourly wage. Include payroll taxes
          (FICA, FUTA, SUTA), workers' comp insurance, health benefits, union contributions if
          applicable, and any per diem or travel costs. A $45/hour mechanic might actually cost you
          $72/hour once you load in the burden.
        </p>
        <p>
          Apply production rates based on your crew's actual performance, not industry averages. If
          your guys hang 30 sheets of drywall a day on a good day, don't bid it at 40. The best
          source is your own closed jobs — that's what
          <a href="/blog/job-costing-for-subcontractors/">job costing</a> gives you: the hours each kind
          of work really took. Build in realistic allowances for mobilization, cleanup, and coordination
          time.
        </p>

        <h2>Step 4: Price Your Materials</h2>
        <p>
          Get current quotes from your suppliers — don't rely on old pricing — and <a
            href="/templates/bid-tabulation/">level them side by side</a
          > before you pick one. Material costs fluctuate, and a number that was good three months ago
          might be 15% higher today. Request quotes with a price hold period so you're covered through
          bid day and the expected award date.
        </p>
        <p>
          Include delivery costs, sales tax (if applicable in your jurisdiction), and any specialty
          items that have long lead times. Flag long-lead items in your proposal so the GC knows
          about potential schedule impacts.
        </p>

        <h2>Step 5: Add Equipment, Subcontractor, and Other Direct Costs</h2>
        <p>
          Don't forget the costs that fall between labor and materials. Rented equipment like lifts,
          scaffolding, or trenchers. Any sub-tier subcontractors you're bringing in. Permits,
          testing, inspection fees, temporary protection, and dumpster costs. These items add up
          quickly and are easy to overlook. Structuring your bid as line items with separate
          material, labor, and other cost buckets — the way <a href="/docs/bids/creating-a-bid/"
            >bids are built in BuildWorkPro</a
          > — makes these stray costs easier to catch.
        </p>

        <Screenshot
          src="/screenshots/05-bid-detail-overview.png"
          alt="Bid detail page in BuildWorkPro showing an accepted HVAC bid with a cost breakdown panel listing estimate total, sales tax, customer total, margin, total materials, people costs, total cost, and company overhead"
          caption="A bid in BuildWorkPro with its cost breakdown panel: materials, people costs, company overhead, and margin tracked as separate buckets — the same cost categories covered in Steps 3 through 6."
        />

        <h2>Step 6: Apply Overhead and Profit</h2>
        <p>
          Your markup needs to cover two things: overhead (the cost of running your business) and
          profit (what you actually take home).
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Overhead</strong> includes office rent, insurance, vehicles, office staff, accounting,
            software, and all the other costs that exist whether you have a job or not. Most subs run
            8-15% overhead depending on their size and structure.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Profit</strong> is your margin after all costs are covered. 5-10% is common for competitive
            bids, though some specialty work or negotiated contracts can support higher margins.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Some contractors roll overhead and profit into a single markup. Others break it out on the
          bid form. Know what the bid documents require and be consistent. <a
            href="/features/construction-bidding/">Bidding software</a
          > that applies margin and overhead as rates across your line items keeps the math consistent
          from one bid to the next.
        </p>

        <h2>A Worked Example: Pricing One Bid</h2>
        <p>
          Here is how the steps add up on a small tenant-improvement scope. The direct costs from
          Steps 2 through 5:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Labor:</strong> 320 hours at a $72 burdened rate = $23,040</li>
          <li><strong>Materials:</strong> supplier quote with delivery and sales tax = $19,900</li>
          <li><strong>Equipment:</strong> a scissor lift for three weeks = $2,400</li>
          <li><strong>Sub-tier:</strong> core drilling = $1,800</li>
          <li><strong>Other direct costs:</strong> permit and dumpster = $860</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          That is <strong>$48,000</strong> of direct cost. Overhead at 12% of direct cost adds $5,760,
          for <strong>$53,760</strong> before profit. To keep an 8% margin on the selling price, divide
          by 0.92 rather than multiplying by 1.08: $53,760 ÷ 0.92 = $58,434.78, so the bid goes out at
          <strong>$58,450</strong>. Multiplying by 1.08 instead would have produced $58,061 — an 8%
          markup, which is only a 7.4% margin. The
          <a href="/blog/construction-markup-vs-margin/">markup vs. margin guide</a> explains why the
          two never match.
        </p>

        <h2>Step 7: Format and Submit Your Proposal</h2>
        <p>
          Presentation matters. A clean, professional proposal tells the GC you run a tight
          operation. Include a clear scope summary that spells out exactly what's included and —
          just as importantly — what's excluded; a
          <a href="/templates/scope-of-work/">scope of work</a> written in the same terms as your takeoff
          makes that easy. List your qualifications, clarifications, and any assumptions you made.
        </p>
        <p>Common items to clarify in your proposal:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Work hours (regular time only, or does the schedule require overtime?)</li>
          <li>Access and staging area assumptions</li>
          <li>Whether temporary power, water, or hoisting is provided by others</li>
          <li>Material price escalation clauses for long-duration projects</li>
          <li>
            Whether bonds are included — and whether the invitation requires a
            <a href="/blog/bid-bonds-vs-performance-bonds/">bid bond</a> with your submission
          </li>
          <li>
            Payment terms and <a href="/blog/aia-pay-application-guide/">retainage</a> expectations
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Submit on time. It sounds obvious, but late bids get thrown out. Build in a buffer — if
          the bid is due at 2:00 PM, have it ready by noon. Use the submission method specified in
          the invitation: email, online portal, or hard copy.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you win, the GC may send a
          <a href="/templates/letter-of-intent/">letter of intent</a> before the formal subcontract. Check
          that its scope and price match your proposal before you countersign — exclusions you wrote into
          the bid have a way of disappearing between award and contract.
        </p>

        <h2>Common Bidding Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Bidding every job.</strong> Be selective. Chasing too many bids spreads your estimating
            team thin and leads to sloppy numbers. Reusable <a href="/docs/bids/templates/"
              >bid templates</a
            > cut estimating time on the jobs you do pursue.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Forgetting mobilization and demobilization.</strong> Getting your crew and equipment
            to and from the site has a real cost.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Ignoring the schedule.</strong> A compressed schedule means overtime, stacking trades,
            and lower productivity. Price accordingly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Not visiting the site.</strong> Plans don't show everything. Site conditions, access
            limitations, and existing conditions can significantly affect your costs.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

